  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Strong to severe thunderstorms are likely today,\nwith some thunderstorm risk lingering into Wednesday.\n\nHigher dew points have been advecting into the area with\nmoisture and instability increasing in S/SW flow. So far shower\nactivity has been isolated and short lived. However, a subtle\nshortwave is approaching, and some of the convection currently\nacross WV and SW VA could persist or redevelop across the area\nthrough the early morning hours. Areas of stratus are also\nforming. Both of these features could inhibit surface heating\nthis morning, although it does appear we should have some\npartial sun by midday as the warm front continues to lift to the\nnorth. This should be enough to allow temperatures to rise into\nthe mid 80s to lower 90s. Combined with dew points in the 70s,\nat least moderate surface based instability should develop,\nhighest east of the Blue Ridge Mountains.\n\nA seasonably strong trough will be approaching from the west, \nproviding forcing and anywhere between 30 and 50 kt of flow in\nthe cloud bearing layer. This will provide a favorable \nenvironment for severe thunderstorms, with supercells and\nclusters both possible. One initiation region will likely be\nalong the prefrontal/lee trough near or just east of the Blue\nRidge. These storms will likely affect the I-95 corridor between\nmid afternoon and early evening. While these storms will\ncertainly carry severe potential, it is interesting to note on\naverage the 850-700 mb wind fields will be greater to our south\nand west until late afternoon or early evening. Other details \nremain unresolved, especially the effects of upstream convection\nthat moved through the upper Great Lakes and mid Mississippi\nValley. This could have some impact on how thunderstorms develop\nacross the Ohio Valley this afternoon and what subsequently\nmoves into western areas. If stronger/more widespread \nconvection does not move in from the Ohio Valley, there is some \npotential there is a relative minimum in coverage between the\nBlue Ridge and Alleghenies.\n\nThe coverage of afternoon storms and upstream effects will\ndetermine what happens this evening as the strongest forcing\noverspread the area along with increasing low level wind fields.\nA second round of showers and thunderstorms will be possible for\nsome areas, though increasing low level stability may limit the\nsevere weather threat. It appears the evening/overnight coverage\ncould gradually shift to southern parts of the forecast area, or\neven mostly to our south, potentially focusing on outflow from\nearlier activity.\n\nDamaging winds will be the primary severe hazard, although \nlarge hail and a tornado or two are possible if supercells \ndevelop. Some localized swaths of more considerable wind damage\nare possible. Relatively speaking, the tornado threat is higher\nto the north (and north of the CWA) closer to the warm front \nand possibly closer to the Chesapeake Bay where low level winds \ncould be backed. With that said, forecast hodographs vary quite \na bit between models and also throughout time, so any relatively\nhigher threat will depend on the mesoscale details.\n\nWith precipitable water values increasing (over 2 inches east of\nthe Blue Ridge), any storm mergers and training along mesoscale\nboundaries could result in localized flash flooding. Overall\nstorm motions will be progressive though, so this threat \nappears too localized for a Flood Watch at the moment and will \ncontinue to be evaluated. Additional areas to monitor will be \nthe Ohio Valley low level jet potentially leading to training \n(looks to be mostly west of the area), and the evening low level\njet leading to backbuilding along any residual \noutflow/convergence feature (best QPF signal is currently mostly\nsouth of the forecast area).\n\nDry air should start working in from the northwest during the\nsecond half of the night, and the surface cold front should\nreach the Appalachians by 8 AM Wednesday. There is still plenty\nof uncertainty regarding the front's progress by afternoon, and\nmore specifically, how far south and east the deeper moisture\nand instability reach. Some models do show convection firing\neast of the Blue Ridge mountains in the afternoon, with shear\nsufficient for severe thunderstorms. SPC has expanded severe\nweather probabilities farther north and west compared to the\nprevious outlook. However, it's also still possible most storms\nare across SE Virginia. Western parts of the CWA will start\nfeeling post frontal effects with falling dew points and highs\nin the upper 70s/lower 80s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Below average temps Thursday through the weekend\nwith only small rain chances.\n\nUL trofing will prevail over NE US with sfc high pressure building \nin settling overhead Fri into Sat. Canadian high pressure will \nresult in lower Tds, allowing for a pleasant airmass in the Mid \nAtlantic for late July. Overnight lows in the 50 for most, low 60s \nmetros and near waters. Would not be surprised with a few pockets of \nmid to upper 40s Thu a",
